      "id": 1,
      "text": "Bradley got its name when the David Bradley Plow Works built a factory there in 1891, and the town renamed itself Bradley City in 1895.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Illinois Central Railroad made Bradley a stop in the 1880s, and the line still runs freight trains through town today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.bradleyillinois.com/towns/bradley.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Bradley once thrived on blue‑collar industry, with Kroehler furniture and a Sears‑owned Roper stove factory as its biggest employers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "When those factories shut in the 1980s, parts of the buildings burned, marking a sharp shift in the town’s economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "By 1990, Bradley had become a regional shopping center, opening Northfield Square Mall and later adding a Wal‑Mart in Bradley Square.",
